WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.680
OK, I got a very, very important question
via voicemail.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:11.860
This is a practical question about long voting
lines for November.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.059
Take a listen to this.

00:00:13.059 --> 00:00:14.449
Very important to know the answer.

00:00:14.449 --> 00:00:17.280
David, I just have a question.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:24.801
Is it true that if you are in line to vote
on voting day or on Election Day, that you

00:00:24.801 --> 00:00:26.920
have to legally be allowed to vote?

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:27.920
So.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:31.200
You know, even if the line is two hours long,
it's Margaret.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.079
You're standing in it before the polls close.

00:00:34.079 --> 00:00:35.640
Are you legally able to vote?

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.480
That's what I'm wondering about.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:39.620
Thank you.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:40.620
OK.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:41.969
Very important question.

00:00:41.969 --> 00:00:48.199
Legally speaking, if you are in line to vote,
by the time the polls close, you must be legally

00:00:48.199 --> 00:00:49.219
allowed to vote.

00:00:49.219 --> 00:00:55.359
The way it's supposed to work is if polls
close at eight o'clock at eight o'clock, someone,

00:00:55.359 --> 00:01:00.609
a poll worker is supposed to go to the end
of the line and sort of be a placeholder for

00:01:00.609 --> 00:01:04.500
the end of the line if you are in front of
the poll worker.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.640
You must be allowed to vote whether it takes
five minutes or five hours or five days because

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.730
you were in line while the polls were open.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:11.730
That's the rule.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.780
You have to get to the polls before they close.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:17.500
If you try to get in line after the polls
have closed, the poll worker would say this

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:18.710
is the end of the line.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:20.830
The polls are now closed.

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:22.040
You're not going to be allowed to vote.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:24.320
That's the way it's supposed to work.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.000
You can weaponize that.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:33.960
You could hypothetically, if you were a bad
actor, you could try to disproportionately

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:42.190
send people to try to spread disinformation
and usher people in line away by saying the

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:43.190
polls have already closed.

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:44.190
You're not going to be allowed to vote.

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:49.360
You could focus that effort in Democratic
precincts, for example, where you're not looking

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.570
specifically at who's in line, but you're
like, okay, this precinct has more Democrats,

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:54.850
this precinct has more Republicans.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:58.780
Let's go to the Democratic precinct and try
to get people to go home and say the polls

00:01:58.780 --> 00:01:59.780
have closed.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:00.780
You're not going to be allowed to vote.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.440
You could do that.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:06.710
And so the point is, yes, if you're in line
before the polls close, you must be allowed

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:07.710
to vote.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:08.869
There may be dirty tricks.

00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:09.869
They may poll stuff.

00:02:09.869 --> 00:02:11.030
Don't fall for it.

